  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Jada received a gift of $180. In the first week, she spent a third of the gift money. She continues spending a third of what is left each week thereafter. </para>
    <para>Which equation best represents the amount of gift money \(g\), in dollars, she has after \(t\)<emphasis> </emphasis>weeks? </para>
    <para> \(g=180−\frac{1}{3}t\) </para>
      <para> \(g=180 \cdot (\frac{1}{3})^t\) </para>
      <para>\(g=\frac{1}{3} \cdot 180^t\) </para>
      <para> \(g=180 \cdot (\frac{2}{3})^t\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>
      \(g=180 \cdot (\frac{2}{3})^t\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <list>
      <item>Jada's initial gift is $180. Then, she spends a third \(\frac{1}{3}\) of the whole amount each week. This means that she has \(\frac{2}{3}\) left after each week because \(1 - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{3} \) (the whole amount minus a third). </item>
      <item>When writing the equation, \(a = 180 \) because that is the starting amount she was given. Then, because she spends \(\frac{1}{3}\) each week, that means only \(\frac{2}{3}\) is leftover. so, \(b = \frac{2}{3}\). The equation that results is \(g = 180 \cdot (23)^t\).</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content>
